        ],
        "text": "there are over 40 theories attempting to decode d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": ", here are some of the most prominent:\n\n1. **The Memory Error / Fact-Checking Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "According to O'Connor, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when brain areas, such as the temporal lobe, send signals to the frontal decision-making regions, indicating tha...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDFtWVzttOWrGrkzyfxoMZdYSCgNh5q6d8tvtIjAE8NAmo3c/sMV0/2v4MLYR6NwMfnxCcTYC1EgEQjE18kBoo8+B44xYjfjzUHNp+UEqFTiyZwZ59suaIfOm5Ml5ifgiFCykfxgE",
            "title": "The Fascinating Science of D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu | Psychology Today",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/brain-reboot/202312/the-fascinating-science-of-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to researcher Akira O'Connor, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when brain areas such as the temporal lobe send signals to the frontal decision-making regions, indicating that an experience is repeating itself. The frontal regions then evaluate the consistency of this signal with past experiences \u2014 and if there is no prior experience, the realization of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n2. **The Delayed Signal Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "In 1965, Robert Efron of Boston's Veterans Hospital proposed that d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by dual neurological processing caused by delayed signals. Efron f...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpIBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDPEfCLYZRO3LClVFNhoMM8vnuAbQD3XXAZQjIjC7rB/BBwmn55InXA97G+vGtnPz7wzqkR2zbzvjgig9pFQTg9LZeSubDCGPRMBXKoUqFm6FRIrjjcWWEQxKw6Mby4lT02CXNvYYBA==",
            "title": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu - Wikipedia",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9j%C3%A0_vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "In 1965, Robert Efron proposed that d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by dual neurological processing caused by delayed signals. The brain's sorting of incoming signals is done in the temporal lobe of the left hemisphere; however, signals enter the temporal lobe twice, once from each hemisphere, normally with a slight delay of milliseconds. Efron proposed that if the two signals were occasionally not synchronized properly, they would be processed as two separate experiences, with the second seeming to be a re-living of the first.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n3. **The Attentional Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "The four most prevalent theories are: Attentional: suggests when our attention is divided, we take in environmental information subconsciously, so tha...",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDMgO8w2QjXjYWvi3SxoMu7rEcFjzTNI3VwFFIjCdhDnx9nCPZx4qvcOvi8TNUckGmAY5+FSJHjNA4+syVOsBmFEGNEzlHhovtTd9PowqE4cjbEu6BYThezS6q4WheS67MGUYBA==",
